---
import SiteLayout from '../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import '../../styles/tldw.css';

const VIDEO_URL = 'https://vimeo.com/1237411633';

function tsLink(ts: string) {
  const parts = ts.split(':').map(Number);
  const [h, m, s] = parts.length === 3 ? parts : [0, parts[0], parts[1]];
  const frag = (h ? `${h}h` : '') + `${m}m${String(s).padStart(2, '0')}s`;
  return `${VIDEO_URL}#t=${frag}`;
}
---
<SiteLayout
  title="TL;DW! Westbrook City Council — October 19, 2026 | Westbrook Currents"
  description="Rec Commission rename sparks the sharpest floor fight in months. Also: a data center ban advances, a new Saccarappa Park deal, and more."
  currentPage="tldw"
>
  <main class="tldw-wrap">
    <a class="help-back" href="/tldw">&#8249; TL;DW!</a>
    <span class="tldw-eyebrow">Special Meeting</span>
    <h1>Westbrook City Council &mdash; October 19, 2026</h1>
    <p class="tldw-meta">Westbrook High School (Room 114), 125 Stroudwater St. (Hybrid via Zoom) &middot; 6:00 PM &middot; 1h 57m total runtime</p>

    <a class="tldw-pdf-link" href="/tldw-2026-10-19-special-meeting.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
      <span><strong>Printable PDF of this summary</strong><span class="sub">Same content, formatted to print or save.</span></span>
      <span class="go">Open &#8599;</span>
    </a>

    <div class="tldw-video-box">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#087ca7" stroke-width="2"><rect x="2" y="5" width="15" height="14" rx="2"></rect><path d="M17 10l5-3v10l-5-3z"></path></svg>
      <a class="watch" href={VIDEO_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Watch the full video on Vimeo &#8599;</a>
    </div>
    <p class="tldw-scrub-note">Each item below shows a timestamp like <b>57:13</b> (minutes:seconds) or <b>1:52:13</b> (hours:minutes:seconds). Click it to jump straight to that moment in the video.</p>

    <p class="intro-line">Quick read: a Saccarappa Park deal and a data center ban both move forward quietly. The real fireworks are below &mdash; a proposal to rename the Recreation &amp; Conservation Commission turned into the sharpest floor fight in months.</p>

    <p class="tldw-section-head">II&ndash;IV. Roll Call, Pledge of Allegiance</p>
    <details class="tldw-item">
      <summary>
        <span class="ti-text">
          <span class="ti-top"><a class="ti-ts" href={tsLink('10:46')} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">10:46</a></span>
          <span class="ti-title">Roll Call &amp; Pledge</span>
          <span class="ti-brief">All seven members present. Quorum established.</span>
        </span>
        <span class="ti-chev">+</span>
      </summary>
      <div class="ti-body">
        <p>President Jennifer Munro called the meeting to order. City Clerk Ellis Ledoux read the land acknowledgement statement and conducted roll call. All seven members &mdash; Councilors McCambridge, Shaughnessy (Vice President), Chau, Turcotte, Faulkingham, Rairdon, and President Munro &mdash; answered present. President Munro led the Pledge of Allegiance. No minutes were presented for approval.</p>
        <span class="tldw-decision">&#10003; Quorum established &mdash; all 7 members present.</span>
      </div>
    </details>

    <p class="tldw-section-head">V. Mayor's Message</p>
    <details class="tldw-item">
      <summary>
        <span class="ti-text">
          <span class="ti-top"><a class="ti-ts" href={tsLink('12:21')} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">12:21</a></span>
          <span class="ti-title">Route 302 Corridor Study</span>
          <span class="ti-brief">A regional safety study, still in its early data-gathering phase.</span>
        </span>
        <span class="ti-chev">+</span>
      </summary>
      <div class="ti-body">
        <p>Chris Chop, Transportation Director for the Greater Portland Council of Governments, presented a regional safety and planning study covering the ~22-mile Route 302 corridor from I-295 in Portland to the Raymond&ndash;Casco town line, done jointly with Portland, Windham, Raymond, and the Maine Department of Transportation. The study is identifying high-crash locations, transit gaps, and candidate projects for future funding; it's still in the public-engagement and data-gathering phase.</p>
        <p>President Munro (Ward 5) noted the Duck Pond Road stretch of Route 302 &ldquo;can be scary at times&hellip; really hazardous.&rdquo; Chop thanked her for the feedback.</p>
        <span class="tldw-decision">&#10003; Informational presentation &mdash; no vote taken.</span>
      </div>
    </details>
    <details class="tldw-item">
      <summary>
        <span class="ti-text">
          <span class="ti-top"><a class="ti-ts" href={tsLink('23:34')} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">23:34</a></span>
          <span class="ti-title">Curbside Brush &amp; Leaf Collection</span>
          <span class="ti-brief">This year's collection schedule for brush and leaves.</span>
        </span>
        <span class="ti-chev">+</span>
      </summary>
      <div class="ti-body">
        <p>Director of Engineering &amp; Public Services Eric Dudley reviewed this year's brush collection schedule (started that day, one pass through the city starting the following Friday) and leaf collection (starting the following Monday, biodegradable bags only, no tape, continuing through roughly early December weather permitting).</p>
        <span class="tldw-decision">&#10003; Informational &mdash; no vote taken.</span>
      </div>
    </details>
    <details class="tldw-item">
      <summary>
        <span class="ti-text">
          <span class="ti-top"><a class="ti-ts" href={tsLink('26:21')} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">26:21</a></span>
          <span class="ti-title">Absentee Voting &amp; Election Day (Nov. 3)</span>
          <span class="ti-brief">Deadlines and extended hours at the Clerk's Office.</span>
        </span>
        <span class="ti-chev">+</span>
      </summary>
      <div class="ti-body">
        <p>City Clerk Ellis Ledoux reviewed absentee-voting deadlines (last day for no-excuse absentee voting: Friday, Oct. 29) and two extended evening hours at the Clerk's Office (Wed. Oct. 21 and Wed. Oct. 28, 7:30 am&ndash;7 pm). Election Day polls at the Westbrook Community Center run 6 am&ndash;8 pm.</p>
        <span class="tldw-decision">&#10003; Informational &mdash; no vote taken.</span>
      </div>
    </details>
    <details class="tldw-item">
      <summary>
        <span class="ti-text">
          <span class="ti-top"><a class="ti-ts" href={tsLink('27:10')} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">27:10</a></span>
          <span class="ti-title">Parking Ordinance Review, Prospect Street, Shaughnessy Recognition &amp; Student-Athlete Statement</span>
          <span class="ti-brief">Three unrelated items covered in one segment.</span>
        </span>
        <span class="ti-chev">+</span>
      </summary>
      <div class="ti-body">
        <p>All part of the same continuous mayor's-message segment (one timestamp covers all three):</p>
        <p><b>Parking/Prospect St.:</b> Mayor Morse announced a staff review of the no-parking schedule against on-the-ground signage, plus an annual report to council on narrower-street parking designations. As part of the Prospect Street reconstruction (narrowing 28&rarr;26 ft for pedestrian/drainage improvements), the north side from Central St. East to the end of Prospect St. becomes no-parking; signs go up by month's end.</p>
        <p><b>Recognition:</b> Mayor Morse recognized Vice President Shaughnessy for the 2026 William F. King Jr. Downtown Champion Award from the Maine Development Foundation, citing his volunteer work with Discover Downtown Westbrook and Friends of the Presumpscot River.</p>
        <p><b>Student-athlete statement:</b> Mayor Morse addressed the recent use of student athletes' photographs in political attacks on social media, calling it &ldquo;wrong&rdquo; and praising the community's &ldquo;overwhelming and nearly universal&rdquo; rejection of the tactic. No council disagreement was voiced.</p>
        <span class="tldw-decision">&#10003; Informational &mdash; no vote taken.</span>
        <p class="label">Action item</p>
        <ul><li>Install no-parking signage on Prospect St. (north side) &mdash; Assignee: Public Services | Deadline: end of October 2026.</li></ul>
      </div>
    </details>

    <p class="tldw-section-head">VI. City Council Messages</p>
    <details class="tldw-item">
      <summary>
        <span class="ti-text">
          <span class="ti-top"><a class="ti-ts" href={tsLink('33:05')} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">33:05</a></span>
          <span class="ti-title">Meeting Start Time (6 pm vs. 7 pm)</span>
          <span class="ti-brief">Turcotte questions the 2024 switch to a 6 pm start.</span>
        </span>
        <span class="ti-chev">+</span>
      </summary>
      <div class="ti-body">
        <p>Councilor Turcotte asked when and why council meetings moved from a 7 pm to a 6 pm start, and whether council had actually voted on it. President Munro explained the 6 pm slot accommodates committee meetings that sometimes precede the council meeting. City Administrator Angela Holmes located the governing ordinance (Order 2024-146, adopted Dec. 16, 2024, amending Code of Ordinances &sect;57-1): regular meetings keep a 6 pm start to allow a preceding committee meeting when needed. Turcotte requested that council consider a 7 pm start on nights without a committee meeting, calling it easier for families; Munro confirmed that is possible.</p>
        <span class="tldw-decision">&#10003; Informational discussion &mdash; no vote taken.</span>
        <p class="label">Action item</p>
        <ul><li>Consider adopting a 7 pm start on nights without a preceding committee meeting &mdash; Assignee: Council / Administrator Holmes | Deadline: none set.</li></ul>
      </div>
    </details>

    <p class="tldw-section-head">VIII. Public Comment (First)</p>
    <details class="tldw-item">
      <summary>
        <span class="ti-text">
          <span class="ti-top"><a class="ti-ts" href={tsLink('35:42')} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">35:42</a></span>
          <span class="ti-title">Recreation &amp; Conservation Commission Rename (preview)</span>
          <span class="ti-brief">Early objections, before the order itself was formally read.</span>
        </span>
        <span class="ti-chev">+</span>
      </summary>
      <div class="ti-body">
        <p>David Von Segern, a departing Rec &amp; Con Commission member speaking only for himself, previewed objections to Order 2026-116 (further below): renaming &ldquo;conservation&rdquo; to &ldquo;sustainability&rdquo;, and sequencing the ordinance rewrite before a new comprehensive plan is adopted. Matt Brunner, the Commission's chair, spoke next with a longer set of objections. See the full point/counterpoint under Order 2026-116 &mdash; both spoke before that order was formally read, which is why their timestamps are earlier than the order's.</p>
        <span class="tldw-decision">&#10003; Public comment &mdash; no vote taken at this stage.</span>
      </div>
    </details>

    <p class="tldw-section-head">X. Orders of the Day (Second &amp; Final Reading)</p>
    <details class="tldw-item">
      <summary>
        <span class="ti-text">
          <span class="ti-top"><a class="ti-ts" href={tsLink('47:30')} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">47:30</a></span>
          <span class="ti-title">Orders 2026-109, 110, 111, 112, 114 &mdash; Consent Block</span>
          <span class="ti-brief">Fire truck bid, asset forfeiture, Mill Brook Preserve, Rock Row Trail, landslide vendor list &mdash; no discussion.</span>
        </span>
        <span class="ti-chev">+</span>
      </summary>
      <div class="ti-body">
        <p>Moved and seconded together by Councilor Rairdon: a $71,476 bid award to Casco Bay Ford for a replacement medium-duty fire department pickup truck; acceptance of $5,433.05 in federal asset-forfeiture funds; a $50,000 open-space-fund expenditure to the Presumpscot Regional Land Trust for Mill Brook Preserve accessibility improvements; a $10,000 open-space-fund expenditure to Portland Trails for the Rock Row Connector Trail; and a vendor-list amendment on the roughly $1.1 million Presumpscot River Corridor landslide vulnerability assessment project (Maine Emergency Management Agency grant plus state match), following the September 2020 landslide. No discussion.</p>
        <span class="tldw-decision">&#10003; Approved as a block by unanimous roll call, 7&ndash;0.</span>
      </div>
    </details>
    <details class="tldw-item">
      <summary>
        <span class="ti-text">
          <span class="ti-top"><a class="ti-ts" href={tsLink('49:36')} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">49:36</a></span>
          <span class="ti-title">Order 2026-113 &mdash; Credere Associates Task Order No. 11 (Riverwalk North)</span>
          <span class="ti-brief">Pulled out of the consent block, funded via an EPA Brownfields grant.</span>
        </span>
        <span class="ti-chev">+</span>
      </summary>
      <div class="ti-body">
        <p>Pulled out of the consent block for separate consideration at Councilor Shaughnessy's request. Councilor Chau moved to amend the order to fund it via an FY24 U.S. Environmental Protection Agency Brownfields Cleanup Grant and to update the associated project vendor list; Shaughnessy seconded. Administrator Holmes explained the vendor list hadn't been updated in some time and needed a few more technical vendors added.</p>
        <span class="tldw-decision">&#10003; Amendment and order approved by unanimous roll call, 7&ndash;0.</span>
      </div>
    </details>

    <p class="tldw-section-head">XI. New Business</p>
    <details class="tldw-item">
      <summary>
        <span class="ti-text">
          <span class="ti-top"><a class="ti-ts" href={tsLink('51:12')} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">51:12</a></span>
          <span class="ti-title">Resolve 2026-18 &mdash; Endorsing Community Development Block Grant Applications</span>
          <span class="ti-brief">Two CDBG applications: a family stability grant and Brown Street design funding.</span>
        </span>
        <span class="ti-chev">+</span>
      </summary>
      <div class="ti-body">
        <p>Administrator Holmes explained the city's two Community Development Block Grant applications: a $70,000 public-service grant for the Intercultural Community Center's Power School Family Stability Initiative (~110 low-income residents served), and a general application for 50% matching funds to update Brown Street reconstruction construction documents, tying in with the Riverwalk North project.</p>
        <span class="tldw-decision">&#10003; Approved by unanimous roll call, 7&ndash;0.</span>
      </div>
    </details>
    <details class="tldw-item" id="order-2026-115">
      <summary>
        <span class="ti-text">
          <span class="ti-top"><a class="ti-ts" href={tsLink('54:44')} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">54:44</a></span>
          <span class="ti-title">Order 2026-115 &mdash; Memorandum of Understanding (MOU) with Quaker Lane Associates, LLC &mdash; Saccarappa Park</span>
          <span class="ti-brief">A new deal tied to the apartments going up next to the park.</span>
        </span>
        <span class="ti-chev">+</span>
      </summary>
      <div class="ti-body">
        <p>Administrator Holmes outlined the MOU covering three items tied to Quaker Lane's adjacent mixed-use development at 7 Kidder Lane (110 market-rate apartments + 5,000 sq. ft. of commercial space): clearing/preparing the utility easement area, extending the boardwalk between Saccarappa Park and Kidder Lane, and installing a new fence (replacing barbed wire) along the fish ladder.</p>
        <span class="tldw-decision">&#10003; Approved by unanimous roll call, 7&ndash;0.</span>
      </div>
    </details>

    <p class="tldw-section-head">Order 2026-116 &mdash; Recreation &amp; Conservation Commission Rewrite</p>
    <details class="tldw-item" id="order-2026-116" open>
      <summary>
        <span class="ti-text">
          <span class="ti-top"><a class="ti-ts" href={tsLink('57:13')} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">57:13</a><span class="ti-hot">&#128293; Feisty</span></span>
          <span class="ti-title">Recreation &amp; Conservation Commission Rename</span>
          <span class="ti-brief">The meeting's real floor fight &mdash; passed 6&ndash;1 after sharp public pushback.</span>
        </span>
        <span class="ti-chev">&#8722;</span>
      </summary>
      <div class="ti-body">
        <p><b>Found on page 77 of the packet.</b> Repeals the existing Recreation &amp; Conservation Commission ordinance (Code of Ordinances Ch. 7, Art. 3) and replaces it with a <i>Recreation &amp; Sustainability Committee</i>: drops 3 alternate seats (7 members retained), removes the state conservation-commission statute reference, sunsets all current members (who may reapply), and &mdash; per a floor amendment from the Oct. 5 Committee of the Whole &mdash; adds a one-year report-back on the new committee's performance.</p>

        <p class="label">Why it's the meeting's one real controversy</p>
        <p>Every other item passed without debate. This one drew two rounds of public comment, a sharp council exchange, a procedural clash with the chair, and the only non-unanimous vote of the night.</p>

        <p class="label">The case against (spoken during general public comment, before this order was formally read &mdash; see timestamps)</p>
        <p class="tldw-quote">&ldquo;I object to the replacement of conservation in the committee to be titled with sustainability. Sustainability is a term that is too often used by companies and government entities in various forms of greenwashing&hellip; These changes under consideration should have followed a new comprehensive plan.&rdquo;<cite>&mdash; David Von Segern, former Rec &amp; Con Commission member (public comment) &middot; <a class="ts-link" href={tsLink('35:42')} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">35:42</a></cite></p>
        <p class="tldw-quote">&ldquo;[We requested a joint workshop with council.] It wasn't [scheduled]. Don't know why, I can guess, but I don't like to assume&hellip; rather than building roadblocks and taking the power of many and consolidating&hellip; those levers of power into the hands of a few. I don't think we like that when we see it a little south of us in D.C.&rdquo;<cite>&mdash; Matt Brunner, Chair, Recreation &amp; Conservation Commission (public comment) &middot; <a class="ts-link" href={tsLink('38:45')} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">38:45</a></cite></p>
        <p class="tldw-quote">&ldquo;Our superpower is suggestion&hellip; our other superpower&hellip; has been holding the administration accountable for some things that were overlooked&hellip; I think to get rid of that check, that balance, is a dangerous and slippery slope.&rdquo;<cite>&mdash; Matt Brunner &middot; <a class="ts-link" href={tsLink('38:45')} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">38:45</a></cite></p>

        <p class="label">The counterpoint</p>
        <p class="tldw-quote">&ldquo;I do believe that at the heart of conservation is sustainability. They are very interchangeable&hellip; I really go and look at the duties and the charge of the committee&hellip; I would feel comfortable with that.&rdquo;<cite>&mdash; Councilor Michael Shaughnessy &middot; <a class="ts-link" href={tsLink('1:16:26')} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">1:16:26</a></cite></p>
        <p>Shaughnessy proposed the amendment ultimately adopted with the order: a one-year report-back on how the new committee performs.</p>
        <p class="tldw-quote">&ldquo;We have been transparent through this whole process&hellip; it's going to be up to the council to decide whether or not you think we've hit the mark.&rdquo;<cite>&mdash; City Administrator Angela Holmes &middot; <a class="ts-link" href={tsLink('1:22:16')} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">1:22:16</a></cite></p>
        <p>Holmes' broader rationale: the ordinance has been in legal tension with the city charter's council/mayor authority since the commission's creation in 1970; the purpose/membership/duties/scope review was applied consistently across every city board and commission, not singled out here; and members may reapply, as happened when the Library Advisory Committee was previously restructured.</p>

        <p class="label">The dissent</p>
        <p class="tldw-quote">&ldquo;I've been observing this administration&hellip; removing a lot of the opportunities for our constituents to have a voice&hellip; this is probably the most disgusting thing I've seen on the council in the 11 years I've been on the council&hellip; I think it's underhanded, I think it's shady, I think it is incredibly disrespectful to the constituents&hellip; I will be voting against this regardless of how it goes.&rdquo;<cite>&mdash; Councilor Anna Turcotte &middot; <a class="ts-link" href={tsLink('1:19:00')} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">1:19:00</a></cite></p>
        <p>Turcotte also clashed procedurally with President Munro, who asked her to &ldquo;focus on the merits of the issue and stop interrupting&rdquo;:</p>
        <p class="tldw-quote">&ldquo;You can't shut me up, okay? I'm speaking&hellip; This is the second time you interrupted me. Point of order. This is disgusting.&rdquo;<cite>&mdash; Councilor Anna Turcotte &middot; <a class="ts-link" href={tsLink('1:21:37')} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">1:21:37</a></cite></p>

        <p class="label">Mayor's rebuttal (defending staff, not re-arguing the policy)</p>
        <p class="tldw-quote">&ldquo;I just want to respond directly to the characterization of the city administration as corrupt, underhanded, or shady. Those are very serious accusations&hellip; an honest disagreement is not evidence of dishonesty. And a different judgment is not evidence of corruption.&rdquo;<cite>&mdash; Mayor David Morse &middot; <a class="ts-link" href={tsLink('1:30:10')} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">1:30:10</a></cite></p>

        <p class="label">Follow-up from the public, after the vote context was set</p>
        <p class="tldw-quote">&ldquo;[Staff said] there's nothing that legally precludes this from happening. It's a fancy way of saying we don't have to do it&hellip; If time sensitivity was important here, I don't think we'd be sunsetting everybody, requiring new applications&hellip;&rdquo;<cite>&mdash; Matt Brunner (second public comment) &middot; <a class="ts-link" href={tsLink('1:35:30')} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">1:35:30</a></cite></p>

        <span class="tldw-decision">&#10003; Approved 6&ndash;1 by roll call [<a class="ts-link" href={tsLink('1:41:51')} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">1:41:51</a>] (Councilor Turcotte dissenting), with Shaughnessy's one-year review amendment attached.</span>
        <p class="label">Action items</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Open a new online application form for the Recreation &amp; Sustainability Committee once adopted &mdash; Assignee: City Administration | Deadline: within the week following adoption.</li>
          <li>Report back to council in one year on the new committee's performance (per Shaughnessy's amendment) &mdash; Assignee: City Administration | Deadline: ~October 2027.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </details>

    <p class="tldw-section-head">XI. New Business (continued)</p>
    <details class="tldw-item">
      <summary>
        <span class="ti-text">
          <span class="ti-top"><a class="ti-ts" href={tsLink('1:42:05')} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">1:42:05</a></span>
          <span class="ti-title">Order 2026-117 &mdash; Mass Gathering Permit &amp; Road Closure (Halloween on Main Street)</span>
          <span class="ti-brief">Main Street closure for the Together Days Committee's Halloween event.</span>
        </span>
        <span class="ti-chev">+</span>
      </summary>
      <div class="ti-body">
        <p>Main Street closure (Spring St. to Bridge St.) 4:15&ndash;6 pm, Friday, Oct. 30, for the Westbrook Together Days Committee's Halloween event. Councilor Chau thanked the volunteer committee for running it without city involvement. Councilor Rairdon raised safety concerns about e-bikes/scooters among pedestrians during the closure; Police Chief Lolly confirmed scooters aren't legally permitted on the roadway/sidewalks, officers will be present (including Capt. Goldberg), and it will be a point of emphasis.</p>
        <span class="tldw-decision">&#10003; Approved by unanimous roll call, 7&ndash;0.</span>
      </div>
    </details>
    <details class="tldw-item">
      <summary>
        <span class="ti-text">
          <span class="ti-top"><a class="ti-ts" href={tsLink('1:48:03')} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">1:48:03</a></span>
          <span class="ti-title">Order 2026-118 &mdash; Bureau of Highway Safety Grant Funds (First Reading)</span>
          <span class="ti-brief">$40,351.60 across five sub-grants for police training and enforcement.</span>
        </span>
        <span class="ti-chev">+</span>
      </summary>
      <div class="ti-body">
        <p>Administrator Holmes detailed five sub-grants totaling the authorized amount ($40,351.60): drug-recognition-expert/forensic-phlebotomist training, impaired-driving campaigns, speed/aggressive-driving enforcement, pedestrian/motor-vehicle enforcement, and crash-reconstruction training. Thanked the police department for consistently pursuing these grants.</p>
        <span class="tldw-decision">&#10003; Passed first reading, unanimous show of hands. Second reading required.</span>
        <p class="label">Action item</p>
        <ul><li>Second and final reading &mdash; Assignee: Council | Deadline: next regular meeting.</li></ul>
      </div>
    </details>
    <details class="tldw-item">
      <summary>
        <span class="ti-text">
          <span class="ti-top"><a class="ti-ts" href={tsLink('1:50:41')} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">1:50:41</a></span>
          <span class="ti-title">Order 2026-119 &mdash; Drainage Easement at 41 Brown Street (First Reading)</span>
          <span class="ti-brief">An easement with Portland Water District to replace a failing culvert.</span>
        </span>
        <span class="ti-chev">+</span>
      </summary>
      <div class="ti-body">
        <p>Authorizes an easement with Portland Water District to replace a failing box culvert (crossing into an unnamed stream feeding the Presumpscot) and maintain it going forward. The city is already under contract with Sebago Technics for the culvert's design.</p>
        <span class="tldw-decision">&#10003; Passed first reading, unanimous show of hands. Second reading required.</span>
        <p class="label">Action item</p>
        <ul><li>Second and final reading &mdash; Assignee: Council | Deadline: next regular meeting.</li></ul>
      </div>
    </details>
    <details class="tldw-item">
      <summary>
        <span class="ti-text">
